WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.179
pay-3 arrives with the amount 12x50.

2
00:00:05.179 --> 00:00:10.137
Somebody's form let a letter through, and parsing it throws, every time.

3
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:16.110
The consumer applies pay-0 to pay-2, hits pay-3 and crashes before it commits.

4
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.406
It restarts at the committed offset, 0, and crashes again.

5
00:00:20.406 --> 00:00:20.999
Forever.

6
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:25.859
The bookmark can't move past pay-3, so everything behind it waits.

7
00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.486
At 30 payments a second, that's 108,000 in an hour.

8
00:00:29.486 --> 00:00:32.045
A record like this is a poison pill.

9
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:33.813
First ask why it failed.

10
00:00:33.813 --> 00:00:39.143
A timeout at the card issuer is transient: the same call can work a second later.

11
00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:43.091
A bad amount is permanent: it fails the same way every time.

12
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:50.446
pay-3 is permanent, so the consumer copies it to a dead-letter topic, with headers saying where it came from and why.

13
00:00:50.446 --> 00:00:54.373
It waits for the broker's acknowledgement, then commits past it.

14
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:58.307
For a transient failure, retry in place a few times.

15
00:00:58.307 --> 00:01:05.491
pay-5's call times out, the consumer waits 100 milliseconds, then 200, and the third attempt works.

16
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:13.247
But if the issuer is down for a whole minute, short waits give up in under a second, and long ones hold the partition for that minute.

17
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.014
So wait somewhere else.

18
00:01:15.014 --> 00:01:22.013
The consumer writes pay-6 to a retry topic with a ten-second delay, commits, and moves on at full speed.

19
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:25.002
A second consumer reads the retry topic.

20
00:01:25.002 --> 00:01:29.500
It pauses until each record is ten seconds old, then tries again.

21
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:33.880
Still failing, pay-6 moves one step down, to a one-minute topic.

22
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.039
By then the issuer is back, and it goes through.

23
00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:39.607
The last step is the dead-letter topic.

24
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.391
Never let one record hold a partition.

25
00:01:42.391 --> 00:01:48.953
Retry briefly in place, wait in retry topics, and park what still fails in a dead-letter topic.
